\ A quotation nested inside another one reads a local of the word that created the outer quotation,
\ after that word has handed the outer quotation to another module in tail position.
\
\ The tail call pops the frame holding the local, so the nested body's own push finds nothing live
\ to close over. It has to inherit the scope of the body it is nested in instead.
\
\ Each word is reached through `[ word ] call` rather than called directly. The failure needs the
\ creating word to run with a module-deps frame that the tail-call loop itself pushed, and a bare
\ call from a statement that continues afterward does not produce one. A one-word quotation body
\ tail-calls into the module, which does, and keeps that out of the hands of line layout.

use "testing" ;
use "./nested_quotation_capture_tail_call_mod.1z" ;

"a nested read survives a module-changing tail call" test-section

[ through-private-helper ] call 0 "a private helper's tail call keeps the local reachable" assert=

[ through-public-helper ] call 0 "a public word in another file keeps it reachable too" assert=

[ through-quotation-valued-local ] call 0 "a callee invoking the block out of a local keeps it reachable" assert=

[ through-helper-binding-its-own-local ] call 0 "a callee's own local does not displace the inherited one" assert=

[ nested-read-prefers-the-local ] call "LOCAL-LABEL" "the inherited local outranks a same-named module word" assert=
